                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "aeb0ee47-1f78-3e83-af4a-c5530e5b11d1",
                        "entry_id": "50fdf7ab-bdec-3906-b9da-bae35379af0e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "niet helemaal droog"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "868ae2e0-b0fc-4cbf-a3d7-d934eb34b780",
                                "sense_id": "aeb0ee47-1f78-3e83-af4a-c5530e5b11d1",
                                "text": "Snijd de roosjes van de bloemkool. Doe ze per twee a drie in een mixer en mix ze kort tot het de structuur heeft van couscous (circa 10 seconden per keer). Het mag niet nattig worden. Doe de couscous in een kom."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "283ba713-c3fd-4c45-958e-e6a6a629f5a2",
                                "sense_id": "aeb0ee47-1f78-3e83-af4a-c5530e5b11d1",
                                "text": "Femke was een van de kinderen die zaterdagmorgen haar arm in het gips liet zetten. Niet dat ze gevallen was of omdat ze zich eens een dagje wilde laten verwennen. Maar gewoon, omdat het kon. Hoe het voelt? \"Wel een beetje raar, warm en een beetje nattig.\""
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "e0e8680c-d496-46dc-ae41-eb01cb6df1a9",
                        "source_entry_id": "50fdf7ab-bdec-3906-b9da-bae35379af0e",
                        "source_sense_id": "aeb0ee47-1f78-3e83-af4a-c5530e5b11d1",
                        "target_lang_word": "moist",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "moist",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8720a717-35bf-3bfd-b7d7-b153b88a39b5",
                                        "entry_id": "a96e2ec9-314e-3d58-98bd-c9fdb2863302",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Characterized by the presence of moisture; not dry; slightly wet; damp."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "35ae23f6-4e51-4471-bb7d-5ba5287df32f",
                                                "sense_id": "8720a717-35bf-3bfd-b7d7-b153b88a39b5",
                                                "text": "Will theſe moyſt Trees,\nThat haue out-liu'd the Eagle, page thy heeles\nAnd skip when thou point'ſt out?"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4791d239-f109-4608-a0aa-b61cd67616c6",
                                                "sense_id": "8720a717-35bf-3bfd-b7d7-b153b88a39b5",
                                                "text": "And [the Angel of the Lord] made the mids of the fornace, as it had bene a moiſt whiſtling wind, ſo that the fire touched them not at all, neither hurt nor troubled them."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "21287bb2-35c6-463c-af02-8a60e106b0b2",
                                                "sense_id": "8720a717-35bf-3bfd-b7d7-b153b88a39b5",
                                                "text": "[Y]et the North-ſide [of the pyramids of Giza] moſt worne, by reaſon of the humiditie of the Northerne wind, which here is the moiſteſt."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9fa29afd-3a4c-4b24-8449-fd1dc1bc993b",
                                                "sense_id": "8720a717-35bf-3bfd-b7d7-b153b88a39b5",
                                                "text": "[W]e ſee Swines and Pigs Fleſh is the Moiſteſt of Fleſhes."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ee9ce3cd-fedc-49e6-9996-1f26e88b5e19",
                                                "sense_id": "8720a717-35bf-3bfd-b7d7-b153b88a39b5",
                                                "text": "Whilſt thee the ſhores, and ſounding Seas\nWaſh far away, where ere thy bones are hurld,\nWhether beyond the ſtormy Hebrides,\nWhere thou perhaps under the whelming tide\nViſit'ſt the bottom of the monſtrous world;\nOr whether thou to our moiſt vows deny'd,\nSleep'ſt by the fable of Bellerus old, […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "85690118-a433-4fe3-b717-ff27d6c74d99",
                                                "sense_id": "8720a717-35bf-3bfd-b7d7-b153b88a39b5",
                                                "text": "[T]he Winds\nBlow moiſt and keen, ſhattering the graceful locks\nOf theſe fair ſpreading Trees; […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "efef10a5-caef-4f74-a3b3-207c0e004a57",
                                                "sense_id": "8720a717-35bf-3bfd-b7d7-b153b88a39b5",
                                                "text": "After every watering, which need not be above twice or thrice in every Summer, ſo they may be thoroughly wet, be ſure to make up the Hills, wherein holes for the water had been made, with ſome parings, and with the weeds, and cooleſt and moiſteſt Materials that can be got."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7c63714e-be69-4af0-bcbd-5a5b6bf12403",
                                                "sense_id": "8720a717-35bf-3bfd-b7d7-b153b88a39b5",
                                                "text": "Nor was it that the figs were moist and pulpy, or that the French plums blushed in modest tartness from their highly-decorated boxes,[…]: but the customers were all so hurried and so eager in the hopeful promise of the day, that they tumbled up against each other at the door, […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "40b2681b-745b-4f1b-9978-a55bba185a75",
                                                "sense_id": "8720a717-35bf-3bfd-b7d7-b153b88a39b5",
                                                "text": "Joseph Smith, a diffident, conscientious young man with moist hands and an awkward, absent-minded manner, was head gardener at Wotton Vanborough."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a77fc657-96c4-41d9-85dd-e2ae1619fb5c",
                                                "sense_id": "8720a717-35bf-3bfd-b7d7-b153b88a39b5",
                                                "text": "'The other car didn't explode,' continues Abu Shujaa. 'The explosives were a bit moist. They had been stored in a place that was too humid.[…]'"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c7901188-bba1-33c0-95ce-928e61353e92",
                                        "entry_id": "a96e2ec9-314e-3d58-98bd-c9fdb2863302",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of eyes: wet with tears; tearful; also (obsolete), watery due to some illness or to old age."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "09024240-9ec3-4b2e-8a70-f951d5e85756",
                                                "sense_id": "c7901188-bba1-33c0-95ce-928e61353e92",
                                                "text": "[H]aue you not a moiſt eie, a dry hand, a yelow cheeke, a white beard, a decreaſing leg, an increaſing belly? […] and will you yet call your ſelfe yong? fie, fie, fie, ſir Iohn."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f4480a9c-c3c4-45a8-973b-d6819f100e37",
                                                "sense_id": "c7901188-bba1-33c0-95ce-928e61353e92",
                                                "text": "Come, the other are conſiderations, when wee come to haue gray heads, and weake hammes, moiſt eyes, and ſhrunke members. Wee'll thinke on 'hem then; then wee'll pray, and faſt."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "aba49e99-11fc-4950-a23b-c06dfee36872",
                                                "sense_id": "c7901188-bba1-33c0-95ce-928e61353e92",
                                                "text": "And on the banner which stood near\nHe glanced a look of holy pride,\nAnd his moist eyes were glorified; […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "dfe125e7-0669-43bb-b867-22ffbac46098",
                                                "sense_id": "c7901188-bba1-33c0-95ce-928e61353e92",
                                                "text": "At this the tender sound of his own voice\nAnd sweet self-pity, or the fancy of it,\nMade his eye moist; but Enid fear'd his eyes,\nMoist as they were, wine-heated from the feast; […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "eb5c8ca0-4d8a-447d-925c-281bc49b141e",
                                                "sense_id": "c7901188-bba1-33c0-95ce-928e61353e92",
                                                "text": "Eyes moist, he [Maurice Stans] hugged one of his attorneys and later said: \"I feel like I've been reborn.\""
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "08779c7c-dd7d-31cb-8f65-83f210d7d015",
                                        "entry_id": "a96e2ec9-314e-3d58-98bd-c9fdb2863302",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of a climate, the weather, etc.: damp, humid, rainy."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c740a437-e651-4055-8b88-d08600567604",
                                                "sense_id": "08779c7c-dd7d-31cb-8f65-83f210d7d015",
                                                "text": "Ye Swains, invoke the Pow'rs who rule the Sky,\nFor a moiſt Summer, and a Winter dry:\nFor Winter drout rewards the Peaſant's Pain,\nAnd broods indulgent on the bury'd Grain."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "dcc45b61-94f1-47b2-b995-8d55335cb45b",
                                                "sense_id": "08779c7c-dd7d-31cb-8f65-83f210d7d015",
                                                "text": "[I]n the year 1752, which we may reckon among ſome of our moiſteſt Summers throughout England, more Rain fell at London than at Plymouth, according to an eſtimate made at both places; […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "048fac2d-ba1f-4d8a-be3b-65ef92661b9a",
                                                "sense_id": "08779c7c-dd7d-31cb-8f65-83f210d7d015",
                                                "text": "The time is early in the evening; the weather moist and raw."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e863a77f-59f4-4a41-84be-f63993341e66",
                                                "sense_id": "08779c7c-dd7d-31cb-8f65-83f210d7d015",
                                                "text": "With its mild, moist climate, Britain is uniquely placed to grow good grass. Through the centuries pastures have produced many of our basic foods including our beef and lamb; our poultry and eggs; our milk[,] butter and cheese."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a0c1ecb1-8e0f-3686-8bf6-f0dd257f790e",
                                        "entry_id": "a96e2ec9-314e-3d58-98bd-c9fdb2863302",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of the vagina: sexually lubricated due to sexual arousal; of a woman: sexually aroused, turned on."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "informal"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "68dd5cf5-e0a6-4979-85e4-20c8112bdf8e",
                                                "sense_id": "a0c1ecb1-8e0f-3686-8bf6-f0dd257f790e",
                                                "text": "He slid a finger in me, checking to make sure I was moist and ready for him."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "536ff9e5-a1a4-3795-b590-637547cc8827",
                                        "entry_id": "a96e2ec9-314e-3d58-98bd-c9fdb2863302",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Characterized by the presence of some fluid such as mucus, pus, etc."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "73a8328f-f01c-3309-837f-813a9598d166",
                                        "entry_id": "a96e2ec9-314e-3d58-98bd-c9fdb2863302",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of sounds of internal organs (especially as heard through a stethoscope): characterized by the sound of air bubbling through a fluid."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "61462520-57be-3097-81d9-3a3c0876c68e",
                                        "entry_id": "a96e2ec9-314e-3d58-98bd-c9fdb2863302",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Pertaining to one of the four essential qualities formerly believed to be present in all things, characterized by wetness; also, having a significant amount of this quality."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "historical"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "baa87a7e-82b6-46bd-8cef-9eefaf5810f7",
                                                "sense_id": "61462520-57be-3097-81d9-3a3c0876c68e",
                                                "text": "[T]he moiſt ſtarre,\nVpon whoſe influence Neptunes Empier ſtands,\nWas ſicke almoſt to doomeſday with eclipſe, […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4c1d5c4d-dbc2-4609-830c-7eb23f561a4f",
                                                "sense_id": "61462520-57be-3097-81d9-3a3c0876c68e",
                                                "text": "[…] Ergo it behooveth then, that the firſt age, and the firſt ſeaſon of things ſhould beginne in the moiſteſt Signe, which is Aries, and in his head, as the principall of the Members, the Fortreſſe of the Soule, and the Signe of Life."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "19e64ea9-ba5b-488b-a7c4-eab1556e17f4",
                                                "sense_id": "61462520-57be-3097-81d9-3a3c0876c68e",
                                                "text": "Pituita, or Fleagme, is a cold and moiſt humour, begotten of the colder part of the Chylus, (or white iuyce comming of the meat digeſted in the ſtomacke) in the Liuer, his office is to nouriſh, and moiſten the members of the body, which as the tongue, are moued, that they be no ouer-drye."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b16eeb2d-0e85-46c0-8045-01ccb4db9306",
                                                "sense_id": "61462520-57be-3097-81d9-3a3c0876c68e",
                                                "text": "He [Aristotle] made four Elements; the firſt, cold and dry; the ſecond, cold and moiſt; the third, hot and moiſt; and the fourth, hot and dry. […] And Water, being the coldeſt and moiſteſt of all Things, he call'd his ſecond Element, Water."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "50c343ab-d007-4e70-95e0-3a3d821996fb",
                                                "sense_id": "61462520-57be-3097-81d9-3a3c0876c68e",
                                                "text": "They [fish] are cold and moiſt, and muſt needs, ſay they, produce juices of the ſame kind, and conſequently are improper to ſtrengthen the body. In this diverſity of opinion, it is the wiſest way to eat our fiſh in the ordinary manner, and pay no great attention to cooks or doctors."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c0dca354-fc10-4e2d-bdb3-a0ed77594a25",
                                                "sense_id": "61462520-57be-3097-81d9-3a3c0876c68e",
                                                "text": "Neither did the live coals, which were lying about in plenty, burn him; for, being a water-baby, his radical humours were of a moist and cold nature, […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "093d1136-a58d-3388-bd7c-d4578418eccf",
                                        "entry_id": "a96e2ec9-314e-3d58-98bd-c9fdb2863302",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Fluid, liquid, watery."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "24305d1f-d180-4259-8c78-66015ccc007b",
                                                "sense_id": "093d1136-a58d-3388-bd7c-d4578418eccf",
                                                "text": "O pardon me, my liege, but for my teares,\nThe moiſt impediments vnto my ſpeech,\nI had foreſtald this deere and deep rebuke, […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6e02b3df-8d09-4e83-9f11-4f68380f797d",
                                                "sense_id": "093d1136-a58d-3388-bd7c-d4578418eccf",
                                                "text": "For the chief, he in whom Pallas pour'd\nHer store of counsels, Ithacus, aboard went last, and then\nThe moist ways of the sea they sail'd."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "297de68b-89a9-4f94-bde6-71bb84ceaf34",
                                                "sense_id": "093d1136-a58d-3388-bd7c-d4578418eccf",
                                                "text": "Some being of the opinion of Thales, that water was the originall of all things, thought it most equall to ſubmit unto the principle of putrefaction, and conclude in a moiſt relentment."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f5451d74-f253-30ce-93aa-c4cb4eed58bb",
                                        "entry_id": "a96e2ec9-314e-3d58-98bd-c9fdb2863302",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Bringing moisture or rain."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "also",
                                            "obsolete",
                                            "poetic"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1de31ec6-e3f5-4593-9d0c-5ee2700e41bc",
                                                "sense_id": "f5451d74-f253-30ce-93aa-c4cb4eed58bb",
                                                "text": "And the moiſt daughters of huge Atlas ſtroue\nInto the Ocean deepe to driue their weary droue."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c1a05ac9-4f43-4a1e-a086-547918aea5bd",
                                                "sense_id": "f5451d74-f253-30ce-93aa-c4cb4eed58bb",
                                                "text": "Ere twice in murke and occidentall dampe\nMoiſt Heſperus hath quench'd her ſleepy Lampe: […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "11419ef9-3c78-47c5-94eb-3357106f9532",
                                                "sense_id": "f5451d74-f253-30ce-93aa-c4cb4eed58bb",
                                                "text": "Nor yet, when moiſt Arcturus clouds the sky,\nThe woods and fields their pleaſing toils deny."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "moist",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4941d328-a5d9-34d8-bf2a-ab215aa2f89d",
                                        "entry_id": "3adb9ef3-256b-314e-8038-9b3a2efaa7c0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Moistness; also, moisture."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "regional",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9780aff8-2e45-4802-b3cb-2c310d42f0ab",
                                                "sense_id": "4941d328-a5d9-34d8-bf2a-ab215aa2f89d",
                                                "text": "[T]hey launch'd the ship, the mast it bore\nAdvanc'd, sails hoised, every seat his oar\nGave with a leather thong. The deep moist then\nThey further reach'd."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fd86d336-fe47-4695-bbc1-6486027ad3bf",
                                                "sense_id": "4941d328-a5d9-34d8-bf2a-ab215aa2f89d",
                                                "text": "Th' Arch-Angel Uriel, one of the ſeav'n\n[…]\nThat run through all the Heav'ns, or down to th' Earth\nBear his [God's] ſwift errands over moiſt and dry,\nO're Sea and Land: […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "moist",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6d610bad-8c84-3d08-a505-4de2f1c3bc9c",
                                        "entry_id": "1ffeaed2-85fc-3ab6-ab06-01efdebfad49",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To make (something) moist or wet; to moisten."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "US",
                                            "regional",
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d3273977-f328-4857-bb08-43212dd851ef",
                                                "sense_id": "6d610bad-8c84-3d08-a505-4de2f1c3bc9c",
                                                "text": "He calleth for a ſponge (ſaith Theodoritus) and therevvith moiſteth and vvaſsheth Simeones mouthe, and then geueth him the holy Sacrament."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "446697d2-cbe1-441c-98d8-086628e961fa",
                                                "sense_id": "6d610bad-8c84-3d08-a505-4de2f1c3bc9c",
                                                "text": "They [the legendary Fortunate Isles] haue raine there very ſeldom, howbeit a gentle winde commonly that bloweth in a litle ſiluer dew, which moiſteth the earth ſo finely, that it maketh it fertile and luſtie, not onely to bring forth all that is ſet or ſowen apon it but of it ſelfe without mans hand it beareth ſo good frute, as ſufficiently maintaineth the inhabitants dwelling apon it, liuing idlely, and taking no paines."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "97de2f79-0b08-4b06-bc59-cda7eb477f0e",
                                                "sense_id": "6d610bad-8c84-3d08-a505-4de2f1c3bc9c",
                                                "text": "Now no more\nThe iuyce of Egypts Grape ſhall moyſt this lip."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "306e84a3-7c9d-3c3f-a21c-0afda6715377",
                                        "entry_id": "1ffeaed2-85fc-3ab6-ab06-01efdebfad49",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To inspire, to refresh (someone); also, to soften (one's heart)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "figuratively",
                                            "obsolete",
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6a0d9bee-d97c-3203-8f0a-5a16a434693c",
                                        "entry_id": "1ffeaed2-85fc-3ab6-ab06-01efdebfad49",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To rain lightly; to drizzle."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "US",
                                            "intransitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b0e59c91-ef2d-3114-b186-57f37b3026fe",
                                        "entry_id": "1ffeaed2-85fc-3ab6-ab06-01efdebfad49",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To have an effect of moistening or wetting."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "intransitive",
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3fb855a4-fe8b-4349-9de6-2bc42f80205c",
                                                "sense_id": "b0e59c91-ef2d-3114-b186-57f37b3026fe",
                                                "text": "Auyſing the bright bemes of theſe fayer Iyes\nwhere he is that myn oft moiſteth & waſſheth",
                                                "translation": "Avising the bright beams of these fair eyes,\nWhere he is that mine oft moisteth and washeth; […]",
                                                "english": "Avising the bright beams of these fair eyes,\nWhere he is that mine oft moisteth and washeth; […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fafe32b7-9b07-46f6-a286-2860b255e864",
                                                "sense_id": "b0e59c91-ef2d-3114-b186-57f37b3026fe",
                                                "text": "Againe, nothyng moiſteth ſoner then water. Therefore, a wepyng eye cauſeth muche moiſture, and prouoketh teares."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a49c1e12-b34f-48b4-8a6d-3decc86041f9",
                                                "sense_id": "b0e59c91-ef2d-3114-b186-57f37b3026fe",
                                                "text": "There is other ſome yͭ [that] heateth temperately. And another yͭ cooleth temperatly, and if moiſtneſſe be ioyned therewith, it moiſteth, and with a drie thinge, it drieth.",
                                                "translation": "Governance of Hygiene of Salerno",
                                                "english": "Governance of Hygiene of Salerno"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9eddd344-006a-495b-b3d3-a9c9ca1d4aeb",
                                                "sense_id": "b0e59c91-ef2d-3114-b186-57f37b3026fe",
                                                "text": "[S]prinkle a vessel of water, and it moisteth not, but cast it out wholly together, and it both washeth and nourisheth. This notable saying, before this time hath encourage Emperors, animated Kings, and allured Princes, to conquer realmes to them adjoining, to vanquish nations to their dominions adjacent, and to subdue people either necessary for their purpose, or being to them daily enemies and continual adversaries."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "0aa90d9c-f803-4b61-ad5c-63aea7a72754",
                        "source_entry_id": "50fdf7ab-bdec-3906-b9da-bae35379af0e",
                        "source_sense_id": "aeb0ee47-1f78-3e83-af4a-c5530e5b11d1",
                        "target_lang_word": "clammy",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "clammy",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2c5390eb-935a-330d-9f90-ea7e2ceefc64",
                                        "entry_id": "61bc5041-fc49-3bd3-9210-4044c6852b3e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Cold and damp, usually referring to hands or palms."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b1f4ab55-8cff-4ed7-a592-2f6dad7e8585",
                                                "sense_id": "2c5390eb-935a-330d-9f90-ea7e2ceefc64",
                                                "text": "Then he could see the modest bookseller, somewhat clammy in his extremities and lost within his academic robe and hood, nervously fidgeting his mortar-board, haled forward by ushers, and tottering rubescent before the chancellor, provost, president (or whoever it might be) who hands out the diploma."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ebb676ed-b4ae-42ef-aaaf-fce29b701cf2",
                                                "sense_id": "2c5390eb-935a-330d-9f90-ea7e2ceefc64",
                                                "text": "His hands were clammy from fright."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d2d1ad26-3bac-4e66-923c-8493f0061f8c",
                                                "sense_id": "2c5390eb-935a-330d-9f90-ea7e2ceefc64",
                                                "text": "The cause is a temperate conglutination ; for both bodies are clammy and viscous , and do bridle the deflux of humours to the hurts , without penning them in too much"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9a6c2166-ca54-498e-9724-4be1c56e7ae6",
                                                "sense_id": "2c5390eb-935a-330d-9f90-ea7e2ceefc64",
                                                "text": "Jenny Lee reached out and took hold of the man’s hand. It was cold and clammy, a deadlike feeling."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5ae1a064-cb82-37d4-99cc-d06dc55c652e",
                                        "entry_id": "61bc5041-fc49-3bd3-9210-4044c6852b3e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The quality of normal skin signs, epidermis that is neither diaphoretic nor dry."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
